WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.926
Today we meet the people schools exist for: the students.

2
00:00:04.926 --> 00:00:09.091
California's students are among the most diverse in the country.

3
00:00:09.091 --> 00:00:10.523
A majority are Latino.

4
00:00:10.523 --> 00:00:17.616
Large numbers are white, Asian American, Filipino, Black, Pacific Islander, Native American, and multiracial.

5
00:00:17.616 --> 00:00:24.970
Roughly one in four is classified as an English learner, and many more grew up speaking another language at home.

6
00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:30.697
Walk into almost any California school and you'll hear several languages in the hallway.

7
00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:35.448
That diversity is a strength, and it also shapes what schools need to do.

8
00:00:35.448 --> 00:00:43.843
A school serving many new English learners needs different staff and supports than one where nearly everyone is a native speaker.

9
00:00:43.843 --> 00:00:48.980
The single biggest factor that predicts how students do in school is not race.

10
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:49.837
It's poverty.

11
00:00:49.837 --> 00:01:05.381
For years, the standard way schools measured that was to count how many students qualified for free or reduced-price school meals, and by that measure, well over half of California's public school students were classified as low-income.

12
00:01:05.381 --> 00:01:21.123
That number doesn't work as cleanly anymore, though, because California has since moved to universal free school meals: every student can now get breakfast and lunch at school at no cost, regardless of family income, no paperwork required.

13
00:01:21.123 --> 00:01:27.973
That's good for kids, but it means the old free-lunch count no longer tracks poverty the way it used to.

14
00:01:27.973 --> 00:01:37.787
Researchers now lean on other measures, like census and tax data, and by those measures, poverty still touches roughly half of California's students.

15
00:01:37.787 --> 00:01:43.649
It remains, by a wide margin, the single biggest predictor of how a child does in school.

16
00:01:43.649 --> 00:01:46.415
Poverty affects learning in concrete ways.

17
00:01:46.415 --> 00:01:56.558
Kids may move often, arrive hungry, sleep poorly in crowded housing, miss school for family responsibilities, or carry stress that makes it hard to focus.

18
00:01:56.558 --> 00:02:02.815
Because poverty in America is tangled up with race, the two often show up together in the data.

19
00:02:02.815 --> 00:02:07.492
Schools can't fix poverty on their own, but they can blunt its effects.

20
00:02:07.492 --> 00:02:09.007
Health is part of that.

21
00:02:09.007 --> 00:02:18.689
A child who can't see the board, has an untreated toothache, or has asthma that flares up at night will struggle no matter how good the teacher is.

22
00:02:18.689 --> 00:02:24.221
That's why many schools offer free meals, vision screenings, nurses, and counseling.

23
00:02:24.221 --> 00:02:25.539
These aren't extras.

24
00:02:25.539 --> 00:02:27.844
They're preconditions for learning.

25
00:02:27.844 --> 00:02:29.353
Then there are parents.

26
00:02:29.353 --> 00:02:35.586
Research is consistent that family involvement matters, but maybe not in the way people assume.

27
00:02:35.586 --> 00:02:38.867
Showing up at bake sales helps a school community.

28
00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:49.562
What helps an individual child most is quieter: talking with them, reading together, setting routines, expecting them to do well, and asking what they're learning.

29
00:02:49.562 --> 00:02:54.680
Parents who don't speak English or who work multiple jobs can do all of these.

30
00:02:54.680 --> 00:03:00.060
Schools that treat parents as partners, not audiences, tend to get better results.

31
00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.168
It's worth pausing on a big idea.

32
00:03:02.168 --> 00:03:10.346
Education is a process where inputs, like money, teacher time, and materials, are supposed to turn into outcomes, like learning.

33
00:03:10.346 --> 00:03:12.582
But the connection isn't automatic.

34
00:03:12.582 --> 00:03:19.738
Two schools with the same budget can get very different results depending on how they use their time and people.

35
00:03:19.738 --> 00:03:21.718
We'll keep coming back to that.

36
00:03:21.718 --> 00:03:24.465
One input that's hard to buy is motivation.

37
00:03:24.465 --> 00:03:31.749
Kids learn more when they believe effort pays off, when they feel they belong, and when the work feels meaningful.

38
00:03:31.749 --> 00:03:33.729
Motivation isn't a fixed trait.

39
00:03:33.729 --> 00:03:40.182
It grows or shrinks depending on what happens in classrooms, and good teachers build it deliberately.

40
00:03:40.182 --> 00:03:53.790
The psychologist Carol Dweck calls this a growth mindset: the idea that ability isn't fixed, that it can be developed through effort, and kids who believe that tend to work harder and recover faster from setbacks.

41
00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.499
Some students need more support by law.

42
00:03:56.499 --> 00:04:06.499
Nationally, roughly one in six students is identified with a disability and receives special education services under a federal law called IDEA.

43
00:04:06.499 --> 00:04:17.820
Each of these students has an Individualized Education Program, or IEP: a written plan, agreed on by the family and the school, that spells out services and goals.

44
00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:24.765
Disabilities range widely, from speech delays and dyslexia to autism and serious medical conditions.

45
00:04:24.765 --> 00:04:34.488
The principle is that every child is entitled to a free, appropriate public education, in the least restrictive setting that works for them.

46
00:04:34.488 --> 00:04:44.836
Special education is also a major and growing cost driver for districts, since schools must serve every eligible student regardless of what it costs.

47
00:04:44.836 --> 00:04:47.614
Other students face disruptions at home.

48
00:04:47.614 --> 00:04:54.768
Children in foster care often change schools several times, losing credits and relationships each time.

49
00:04:54.768 --> 00:05:04.838
California law gives foster youth specific rights, like staying in their original school when possible and getting credit for partial coursework.

50
00:05:04.838 --> 00:05:09.213
Undocumented students have a right to attend public school too.

51
00:05:09.213 --> 00:05:09.977
A 1982 U.S.

52
00:05:09.977 --> 00:05:12.269
Supreme Court decision, Plyler v.

53
00:05:12.269 --> 00:05:18.936
Doe, held that states can't deny children a free public education because of immigration status.

54
00:05:18.936 --> 00:05:22.409
Schools generally aren't supposed to ask about it.

55
00:05:22.409 --> 00:05:26.096
Finally, students aren't just recipients of education.

56
00:05:26.096 --> 00:05:27.667
They can help shape it.

57
00:05:27.667 --> 00:05:35.314
California school boards can include student members, and the State Board of Education has a student member too.

58
00:05:35.314 --> 00:05:40.230
Students serve on school site councils and give input on district plans.

59
00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:47.058
When young people are asked what's working and what isn't, they often point to problems adults miss.

60
00:05:47.058 --> 00:05:51.565
To go deeper, see Chapter 2 of the free Ed100 course at ed100.org.
